WEBVTT

00:00.070 --> 00:03.065
Data logistics, the work we do in my team,

00:03.065 --> 00:06.112
is designing tools

00:06.112 --> 00:08.897
and radio astronomy

00:08.897 --> 00:11.682
for us is a playground for experimentation.

00:11.682 --> 00:14.729
You could see it as designing

00:14.729 --> 00:17.777
a beach rake, a shovel, a bucket,

00:17.777 --> 00:20.772
and our sandbox is ECLAT,

00:20.772 --> 00:23.662
we're going to play with the sand to build things and see if they stand up.

00:25.350 --> 00:28.995
My name is Mathis Certenais, I'm 25 years old,

00:28.995 --> 00:32.451
and I joined ESIR, an engineering school in Rennes.

00:32.451 --> 00:35.845
After that, I decided to do a double degree in Canada

00:35.845 --> 00:39.238
for a year on subjects related to artificial intelligence,

00:39.238 --> 00:42.946
the cloud and more advanced computer science concepts.

00:42.946 --> 00:46.403
After that, I came back to Brittany to work on

00:46.403 --> 00:50.110
a project related to logistics.

00:50.110 --> 00:53.755
These questions didn't necessarily arise before

00:53.755 --> 00:55.190
because everyone did it on their own.

00:55.190 --> 00:58.571
We collected the data, processed it

00:58.571 --> 01:01.839
and analysed it locally.

01:01.839 --> 01:04.995
But with the new generation of radio telescopes,

01:04.995 --> 01:07.700
we're going to have an explosion in data volumes

01:07.700 --> 01:10.969
and we need to adapt and integrate
this new imaging technique into supercomputers

01:20.395 --> 01:25.280
and, above all,

01:25.280 --> 01:27.670
distribute the processing of these information

01:27.670 --> 01:31.005
flows across different architectures infrastructures

01:31.005 --> 01:33.944
in order to answer questions a

01:33.944 --> 01:37.280
and solve problems

01:37.280 --> 01:40.615
related to this much larger flow of information.

01:40.615 --> 01:43.837
The objective of ECLAT is to design cyber-physical systems,

01:43.837 --> 01:47.003
the workflows,

01:47.003 --> 01:50.394
for the radio astronomy of tomorrow,

01:50.394 --> 01:53.240
in particular the SKAO.

01:58.390 --> 02:01.593
NumPEx brings together

02:01.593 --> 02:04.851
five groups of engineers,

02:04.851 --> 02:07.990
PhD and researchers working on

02:07.990 --> 02:12.173
the digital issues of tomorrow.

02:12.173 --> 02:16.148
And Youngpex

02:16.148 --> 02:20.332
brings together all young people to work

02:20.332 --> 02:22.480
on topics

02:22.480 --> 02:25.255
that are very relevant to our generation.

02:25.255 --> 02:28.081
questions qu'on va devoir répondre pour pour les nous de

02:28.081 --> 02:30.906
We've focused on two topics:

02:30.906 --> 02:33.484
energy footprint issues.

02:33.484 --> 02:36.409
How we're going to do research

02:36.409 --> 02:39.310
in an eco-responsible way.

02:39.310 --> 02:42.700
And the second thing

02:42.700 --> 02:45.918
is multidisciplinarity.

02:45.918 --> 02:49.251
It's a time in your life

02:49.251 --> 02:52.354
when you specialise in a subject.

02:52.354 --> 02:55.572
I find the human side of it really incredible.

02:55.572 --> 02:58.963
Joining a team with specialists

02:58.963 --> 03:02.296
and working within ECLAT with people

03:02.296 --> 03:05.640
who have completely different backgrounds.
